WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.620
Hey there, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners,

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.960
episode 92 for July 4th, 2026.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.640
Some awesome AI developments today,

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:10.760
and we're going to make all of it make sense.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:12.100
Let's get into it.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.220
Open AI's co-founder just shared a vision

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.380
where future tools might remove every button, menu,

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.240
and app screen you normally tap.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.440
Instead of learning new software step-by-step,

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:25.940
the system would watch what you are already doing

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.680
and handle the details quietly.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.660
Think of it like a helpful neighbor who notices

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.780
your trash day and wheels the bin out

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.140
before you even remember.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:38.220
Right now, most chatbots still wait for you to type a command,

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:41.600
but the goal is an AI that already knows your patterns

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.560
and steps in at the right moment.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.460
This matters for students because every new website or app

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.860
takes time to figure out during busy weeks.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:52.560
If the AI removes that learning curve,

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.720
planning a group project or finding research sources

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.020
could feel much simpler.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.860
The shift also raises a bigger question

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.820
about which skills stay valuable

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.600
once the software itself becomes less visible.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.880
You can already test the direction

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.300
by opening chat GPT on your phone

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.420
and holding longer, more natural back and forth talks

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:13.840
instead of short commands.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.880
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show,

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.880
the deep dive into how one idea actually works.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.500
Today's deep dive looks at the invisible helper vision

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:24.500
we just heard about.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:26.840
Start with something you already know.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.240
Your phone's keyboard guesses the next word while you text.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:32.840
Now, picture that same guessing system

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:35.540
watching everything else you do, not just the letters.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.680
First, it reads the situation,

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:39.480
like seeing a calendar deadline

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.880
and a bus search open at the same time.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.380
Next, it predicts what would help next,

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.880
such as booking the ride or adding a reminder.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.820
Finally, it acts without extra buttons

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.480
and the more it sees your habits,

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.240
the sharper those guesses become.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:57.260
What feels like magic is really just very good prediction

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.220
running in the background the whole time.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:02.980
That is the idea behind the almost no interface future

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:04.160
people are talking about

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.340
and you now have a clear picture of the steps it would take.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:10.880
Let's move to some cool stuff you can try right now.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.040
First up is a new glossary from TechCrunch

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:17.220
that turns confusing AI words into plain language.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.200
It explains terms like hallucinations

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.300
when an AI makes up facts that sound believable

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:24.140
and tokens.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.180
The small chunks of text, the model reads.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.960
Go to the TechCrunch site, pick three words you have heard

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.160
before and read their short definitions.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.160
Then open any chatbot and try using one of those words

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.260
in a question to see whether the explanation

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:40.600
clicks in real time.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.560
Next, websites are testing ways to replace those annoying

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.000
setgeas that ask you to click traffic lights

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.420
or type distorted letters.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.520
AI has gotten so good at solving them

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.640
that some sites are trying other checks

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:54.980
to confirm you are human.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:58.060
Visit a page that still shows a capture

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:00.140
and notice how fast you finish it.

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.500
Then imagine how the experience might change

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.220
when the puzzles start to disappear.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.720
Here are two quick bits to finish.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:11.340
A study of over 26,000 students found that those who used AI

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.760
finished homework faster and earned better daily grades

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.620
yet scored up to 24% lower on major exams two years later.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.920
The gap only appeared after enough time had passed

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.800
for the missing practice to show up.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.360
Researchers tracked the same group across multiple years

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:30.360
to isolate the long-term effect.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.640
Daily assignments showed immediate gains in speed

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.040
and short-term scores.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.460
Exam performance, however, revealed the hidden cost

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.760
once cumulative practice was missed.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.860
The findings come from the decoder.com

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:46.120
and highlight how short-term efficiency

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:47.880
can mask longer skill gaps.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:51.060
Students and teachers can review the full report

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:54.440
to weigh daily tool use against exam preparation needs.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.460
In other news, the company behind Claude

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:00.160
is starting drug discovery projects for diseases

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.800
that big pharmaceutical firms often skip

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:04.380
because they are not profitable.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:08.200
Early plans suggest AI could shorten the usual

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:11.640
12-year process and improve success rates along the way.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:15.380
Andthropic is the firm launching these targeted efforts.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.420
The focus stays on conditions large drug makers

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.680
pass over due to low market returns.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.680
AI models would handle early screening

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:27.260
and design steps that traditionally stretch across many years.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.360
The announcement appears on the verge.com

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.860
and points to measurable time savings in the initial phases.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:37.280
Observers note the approach could open research path

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:38.980
that were previously set aside.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:40.300
And that's a wrap.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.560
If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:44.940
Go play with it.

